          "text": "I2C manual (https://www.nxp.com/docs/en/application-note/AN10216.pdf)\nciteturn4view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 51; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.nxp.com/docs/en/application-note/AN10216.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 5786\nL0@P0: AN10216-01 I INTEGRATED CIRCUITS\nL1@P0: 2\nL2@P0: C Manual\nL3@P0: 1\nL4@P0: APPLICATION NOTE\nL5@P0: AN10216-01\nL6@P0: I\nL7@P0: 2\nL8@P0: C MANUAL\nL9@P0: Abstract – The I2C Manual provides a broad overview of the various serial buses,\nL10@P0: why the I2\nL11@P0: C bus should be considered, technical detail of the I2C bus and how it\nL12@P0: works, previous limitations/solutions, comparison to the SMBus, Intelligent Platform\nL13@P0: Management Interface implementations, review of the different I2\nL14@P0: C devices that are\nL15@P0: available and patent/royalty information. The I2\nL16@P0: C Manual was presented during the 3\nL17@P0: hour TecForum at DesignCon 2003 in San Jose, CA on 27 January 2003.\nL18@P0: Jean-Marc Irazabal – I\nL19@P0: 2\nL20@P0: C Technical Marketing Manager\nL21@P0: Steve Blozis – I\nL22@P0: 2\nL23@P0: C International Product Manager\nL24@P0: Specialty Logic Product Line\nL25@P0: Logic Product Group\nL26@P0-1: Philips Semiconductors March 24, 2003 AN10216-01 I2\nL27@P1: C Manual\nL28@P1: TABLE OF CONTENTS\nL29@P1: TABLE OF CONTENTS...................................................................................................................................................2\nL30@P1: OVERVIEW .......................................................................................................................................................................4\nL31@P1: DESCRIPTION.....................................................................................................................................................................4\nL32@P1: SERIAL BUS OVERVIEW...............................................................................................................................................4\nL33@P1: UART OVERVIEW.............................................................................................................................................................6\nL34@P1: SPI OVERVIEW..................................................................................................................................................................6\nL35@P1: CAN OVERVIEW...............................................................................................................................................................7\nL36@P1: USB OVERVIEW................................................................................................................................................................9\nL37@P1: 1394 OVERVIEW .............................................................................................................................................................10\nL38@P1: I\nL39@P1: 2\nL40@P1: C OVERVIEW ................................................................................................................................................................11\nL41@P1: SERIAL BUS COMPARISON SUMMARY .............................................................................................................................12\nL42@P1: I\nL43@P1: 2\nL44@P1: C THEORY OF OPERATION ....................................................................................................................................13\nL45@P1: I\nL46@P1: 2\nL47@P1: C BUS TERMINOLOGY...................................................................................................................................................13\nL48@P1: START AND STOP CONDITIONS ....................................................................................................................................14\nL49@P1: HARDWARE CONFIGURATION ...............................................................................................................................14\nL50@P1: BUS COMMUNICATION.............................................................................................................................................14\nL51@P1: TERMINOLOGY FOR BUS TRANSFER ................................................................................................................................15\nL52@P1: I\nL53@P1: 2\nL54@P1: C DESIGNER BENEFITS .................................................................................................................................................17\nL55@P1: I\nL56@P1: 2\nL57@P1: C MANUFACTURERS BENEFITS.....................................................................................................................................17\nL58@P1: OVERCOMING PREVIOUS LIMITATIONS .............................................................................................................18\nL59@P1: ADDRESS CONFLICTS ......................................................................................................................................................18\nL60@P1: CAPACITIVE LOADING > 400 PF (ISOLATION) .................................................................................................................19\nL61@P1: VOLTAGE LEVEL TRANSLATION .....................................................................................................................................20\nL62@P1: INCREASE I\nL63@P1: 2\nL64@P1: C BUS RELIABILITY (SLAVE DEVICES).........................................................................................................21\nL65@P1: INCREASING I\nL66@P1: 2\nL67@P1: C BUS RELIABILITY (MASTER DEVICES)..................................................................................................22\nL68@P1: CAPACITIVE LOADING > 400 PF (BUFFER)......................................................................................................................22\nL69@P1: LIVE INSERTION INTO THE I\nL70@P1: 2\nL71@P1: C BUS .................................................................................................................................24\nL72@P1: LONG I\nL73@P1: 2\nL74@P1: C BUS LENGTHS .................................................................................................................................................25\nL75@P1: PARALLEL TO I\nL76@P1: 2\nL77@P1: C BUS CONTROLLER ..............................................................................................................................25\nL78@P1: DEVELOPMENT TOOLS AND EVALUATION BOARD OVERVIEW..................................................................26\nL79@P1: PURPOSE OF THE DEVELOPMENT TOOL AND I\nL80@P1: 2\nL81@P1: C EVALUATION BOARD...........................................................................26\nL82@P1: WIN-I2CNT SCREEN EXAMPLES.....................................................................................................................................28\nL83@P1: HOW TO ORDER THE I2C 2002-1A EVALUATION KIT.....................................................................................................31\nL84@P1: COMPARISON OF I\nL85@P1: 2\nL86@P1: C WITH SMBUS........................................................................................................................31\nL87@P1: I\nL88@P1: 2\nL89@P1: C/SMBUS COMPLIANCY ...............................................................................................................................................31\nL90@P1: DIFFERENCES SMBUS 1.0 AND SMBUS 2.0 ....................................................................................................................32\nL91@P1: INTELLIGENT PLATFORM MANAGEMENT INTERFACE (IPMI)....................................................................32\nL92@P1: INTEL SERVER MANAGEMENT.........................................................................................................................................33\nL93@P1: PICMG ...........................................................................................................................................................................33\nL94@P1: VMEBUS.........................................................................................................................................................................34\nL95@P1: I\nL96@P1: 2\nL97@P1: C DEVICE OVERVIEW..............................................................................................................................................35\nL98@P1: TV RECEPTION................................................................................................................................................................36\nL99@P1: RADIO RECEPTION ..........................................................................................................................................................36\nL100@P1-2: 2 AN10216-01 I2\nL101@P2: C Manual\nL102@P2: AUDIO PROCESSING ........................................................................................................................................................37\nL103@P2: DUAL TONE MULTI-FREQUENCY (DTMF)......................................................................................................................37\nL104@P2: LCD DISPLAY DRIVER....................................................................................................................................................37\nL105@P2: LIGHT SENSOR ................................................................................................................................................................38\nL106@P2: REAL TIME CLOCK/CALENDAR.......................................................................................................................................38\nL107@P2: GENERAL PURPOSE I/O EXPANDERS ...............................................................................................................................38\nL108@P2: LED DIMMERS AND BLINKERS .......................................................................................................................................40\nL109@P2: DIP SWITCH....................................................................................................................................................................42\nL110@P2: MULTIPLEXERS AND SWITCHES.......................................................................................................................................43\nL111@P2: VOLTAGE LEVEL TRANSLATORS.....................................................................................................................................45\nL112@P2: BUS REPEATERS AND HUBS ............................................................................................................................................45\nL113@P2: HOT SWAP BUS BUFFERS ................................................................................................................................................45\nL114@P2: BUS EXTENDERS .............................................................................................................................................................46\nL115@P2: ELECTRO-OPTICAL ISOLATION........................................................................................................................................47\nL116@P2: RISE TIME ACCELERATORS .............................................................................................................................................47\nL117@P2: PARALLEL BUS TO I\nL118@P2: 2\nL119@P2: C BUS CONTROLLER ......................................................................................................................48\nL120@P2: DIGITAL POTENTIOMETERS .............................................................................................................................................48\nL121@P2: ANALOG TO DIGITAL CONVERTERS ................................................................................................................................48\nL122@P2: SERIAL RAM/EEPROM .................................................................................................................................................49\nL123@P2: HARDWARE MONITORS/TEMP & VOLTAGE SENSORS .....................................................................................................49\nL124@P2: MICROCONTROLLERS ......................................................................................................................................................49\nL125@P2: I\nL126@P2: 2\nL127@P2: C PATENT AND LEGAL INFORMATION..............................................................................................................50\nL128@P2: ADDITIONAL INFORMATION ...................................................................................................................................50\nL129@P2: APPLICATION NOTES..................................................................................................................................................50\nL130@P2-3: 3 AN10216-01 I2\nL131@P3: C Manual\nL132@P3: OVERVIEW\nL133@P3: Description\nL134@P3: Philips Semiconductors developed the I2C bus over 20 years ago and has an extensive collection of specific use and\nL135@P3: general purpose devices. This application note was developed from the 3 hour long I\nL136@P3: 2\nL137@P3: C Overview TecForum presentation\nL138@P3: at DesignCon 2003 in San Jose, CA on 27 January 2003 and provides a broad overview of how the I\nL139@P3: 2\nL140@P3: C bus compares to\nL141@P3: other serial buses, how the I\nL142@P3: 2\nL143@P3: C bus works, ways to overcome previous limitations, new uses of I2C such as in the\nL144@P3: Intelligent Platform Management Interface, overview of the various different categories of I\nL145@P3: 2\nL146@P3: C devices and patent/royalty\nL147@P3: information. Full size Slides are posted as a PDF file on the Philips Logic I2C collateral web site as DesignCon 2003\nL148@P3: TecForum I2C Bus Overview PDF file. Place holder and title slides have been removed from this application note and\nL149@P3: some slides with all text have been incorporated into the application note speaker notes.\nL150@P3: Serial Bus Overview\nL151@P3: DesignCon 2003 TecForum I\nL152@P3: 2C Bus Overview 5\nL153@P3: B U S\nL154@P3: Consumer\nL155@P3: Communications\nL156@P3: IEEE1394\nL157@P3: Industrial\nL158@P3: SERIAL\nL159@P3: BUSES\nL160@P3: SPI\nL161@P3: UART\nL162@P3: Automotive\nL163@P3: Slide 5\nL164@P3: DesignCon 2003 TecForum I\nL165@P3: 2C Bus Overview 6\nL166@P3: General concept for Serial communications\nL167@P3: SCL\nL168@P3: SDA\nL169@P3: • Notice that Slave 1 cannot communicate with Slave 2 or 3 (except via the ‘master’)\nL170@P3: Only the ‘master’ can start communicating. Slaves can ‘only speak when spoken to’\nL171@P3: • Data, Select and R/W signals can share the same line, depending on the protocol\nL172@P3: • An asynchronous communication does not have a Clock signal\nL173@P3: • A point to point communication does not require a Select control signal\nL174@P3: DA “MASTER” SLAVE 1 SLAVE 2 SLAVE 3 TA Parallel to Serial Shift Register\nL175@P3: enable enable enable\nL176@P3: select 3\nL177@P3: select 2\nL178@P3: select 1\nL179@P3: R/W R/W R/W\nL180@P3: READ\nL181@P3: or\nL182@P3: WRITE? // to Ser.\nL183@P3: Shift Reg#\nL184@P3: // to Ser.\nL185@P3: Shift Reg#\nL186@P3: // to Ser.\nL187@P3: Shift Reg#\nL188@P3: Slide 6\nL189@P3: Buses come in two forms, serial and parallel. The data\nL190@P3: and/or addresses can be sent over 1 wire, bit after bit, or\nL191@P3: over 8 or 32 wires at once. Always there has to be some\nL192@P3: way to share the common wiring, some rules, and some\nL193@P3: synchronization. Slide 6 shows a serial data bus with\nL194@P3: three shared signal lines, for bit timing, data, and R/W.\nL195@P3: The selection of communicating partners is made with\nL196@P3: one separate wire for each chip. As the number of chips\nL197@P3: grows, so do the selection wires. The next stage is to\nL198@P3: use multiplexing of the selection wires and call them an\nL199@P3: address bus.\nL200@P3: If there are 8 address wires we can select any one of\nL201@P3: 256 devices by using a ‘one of 256’ decoder IC. In a\nL202@P3: parallel bus system there could be 8 or 16 (or more)\nL203@P3: data wires. Taken to the next step, we can share the\nL204@P3: function of the wires between addresses and data but it\nL205@P3: starts to take quite a bit of hardware and worst is, we\nL206@P3: still have lots of wires. We can take a different\nL207@P3: approach and try to eliminate all except the data wiring\nL208@P3: itself. Then we need to multiplex the data, the selection\nL209@P3: (address), and the direction info - read/write. We need\nL210@P3: to develop relatively complex rules for that, but we save\nL211@P3: on those wires. This presentation covers buses that use\nL212@P3: only one or two data lines so that they are still attractive\nL213@P3: for sending data over reasonable distances - at least a\nL214@P3: few meters, but perhaps even km.\nL215@P3: DesignCon 2003 TecForum I\nL216@P3: 2C Bus Overview 7\nL217@P3: Typical Signaling Characteristics\nL218@P3: I\nL219@P3: 2C\nL220@P3: GTL+\nL221@P3: LVT\nL222@P3: LVC\nL223@P3: 5 V\nL224@P3: 1394\nL225@P3: CML\nL226@P3: RS422/485\nL227@P3: PECL LVPECL LVDS\nL228@P3: GTL\nL229@P3: GTLP\nL230@P3: LVTTL\nL231@P3: I\nL232@P3: 2C\nL233@P3: 3.3 V 2.5 V\nL234@P3: SMBus\nL235@P3: I\nL236@P3: 2C\nL237@P3: Slide 7\nL238@P3: Devices can communicate differentially or single ended\nL239@P3: with various signal characteristics as shown in Slide 7.\nL240@P3-4: 4 AN10216-01 I2\nL241@P4: C Manual\nL242@P4: DesignCon 2003 TecForum I\nL243@P4: 2C Bus Overview 8\nL244@P4: Transmission Standards\nL245@P4: General\nL246@P4: Purpose\nL247@P4: Logic\nL248@P4: GTLP\nL249@P4: BTL\nL250@P4: ETL\nL251@P4: 1394.a\nL252@P4: CML\nL253@P4: RS-422\nL254@P4: RS-485\nL255@P4: RS-232 RS-423\nL256@P4: LVDS =RS-644\nL257@P4: ECL/PECL/LVPECL\nL258@P4: I\nL259@P4: 2C\nL260@P4: 0.1\nL261@P4: 1\nL262@P4: 10\nL263@P4: 35\nL264@P4: 400\nL265@P4: 655\nL266@P4: 2500\nL267@P4: Data Transfer Rate (Mbps)\nL268@P4: 0.5 0 10 100 1000\nL269@P4: Backplane Length (meters) Cable Length (meters)\nL270@P4: Slide 8\nL271@P4: The various data transmission rates vs length or cable\nL272@P4: or backplane length of the different transmission\nL273@P4: standards are shown in Slide 8.\nL274@P4: DesignCon 2003 TecForum I\nL275@P4: 2C Bus Overview 9\nL276@P4: Speed of various connectivity methods (bits/sec)\nL277@P4: I\nL278@P4: 2\nL279@P4: C (‘Industrial’, and SMBus)\nL280@P4: SPI\nL281@P4: 100 kHz\nL282@P4: 110 kHz (original speed)\nL283@P4: I\nL284@P4: 2\nL285@P4: C\nL286@P4: I\nL287@P4: 2\nL288@P4: C ‘High Speed mode’\nL289@P4: 400 kHz\nL290@P4: 3.4 MHz\nL291@P4: USB (1.1) 1.5 MHz or 12 MHz\nL292@P4: Firewire / IEEE1394 400 MHz\nL293@P4: Hi-Speed USB (2.0) 480 MHz\nL294@P4: CAN (1 Wire) 33 kHz (typ)\nL295@P4: CAN (fault tolerant) 125 kHz\nL296@P4: CAN (high speed) 1 MHz\nL297@P4: SCSI (parallel bus) 40 MHz\nL298@P4: Fast SCSI 8-80 MHz\nL299@P4: Ultra SCSI-3 18-160 MHz\nL300@P4: Slide 9\nL301@P4: Increasing fast serial transmission specifications are\nL302@P4: shown in Slide 9. Proper treatment of the 480 MHz\nL303@P4: version of USB - trying to beat the emerging 400 MHz\nL304@P4: 1394a spec - that is looking to an improved ‘b’ spec - -\nL305@P4: etc is beyond the scope of this presentation. Philips is\nL306@P4: developing leading-edge components to support both\nL307@P4: USB and 1394 buses.\nL308@P4: Today the path forward in USB is built on “OTG” (On\nL309@P4: The Go) applications but the costs and complexity of\nL310@P4: this are probably beyond the limits of many customers.\nL311@P4: If designers are identified as designing for large\nL312@P4: international markets then please contact the USB\nL313@P4: group for additional support, particularly of Host and\nL314@P4: OTG solutions. Apologies for inclusion of the parallel\nL315@P4: SCSI bus. It is intended for comparison purposes and\nL316@P4: also because it may be used within the PC software as a\nL317@P4: general data path that USB drivers can use.\nL318@P4: Terminology for USB: The use of older terms such as\nL319@P4: the spec version 1.1 and 2.0 is now discouraged. There\nL320@P4: is just “USB” (meaning the original 12 Mbits/sec and\nL321@P4: 1.5 Mbits/sec speeds of USB version 1.1) and Hi-Speed\nL322@P4: USB meaning the faster 480 Mbits/sec option included\nL323@P4: in spec version 2.0. Parts conforming to or capable of\nL324@P4: the 480 Mbits/sec are certified as Hi-Speed USB and\nL325@P4: will then feature the logo with the red stripe “Hi-Speed”\nL326@P4: fitted above the standard USB logo. The reason to avoid\nL327@P4: use of the new spec version 2.0 as a generic name is\nL328@P4: that this version includes all the older versions and\nL329@P4: speeds as well as the new Hi-Speed specs. So USB 2.0\nL330@P4: compliance does NOT imply Hi-Speed (480 Mbits/sec).\nL331@P4: ICs can be compliant with USB 2.0 specifications yet\nL332@P4: only be capable of the older ‘full speed’ or 12\nL333@P4: Mbits/sec.\nL334@P4: DesignCon 2003 TecForum I\nL335@P4: 2C Bus Overview 10\nL336@P4: Bus characteristics compared\nL337@P4: Bus Data rate\nL338@P4: (bits / sec)\nL339@P4: Length\nL340@P4: (meters) Length limiting f actor\nL341@P4: Nodes\nL342@P4: Typ.number\nL343@P4: Node number\nL344@P4: limiting factor\nL345@P4: I2C 400k 2 w iring capacitance 20 400pF max\nL346@P4: I\nL347@P4: 2C w ith buf fer 400k 100 propagation delays any no limit\nL348@P4: I2C high speed 3.4M 0.5 w iring capacitance 5 100pF max\nL349@P4: CAN 1 wire 33k 100 total capacitance 32\nL350@P4: 5k 10km\nL351@P4: 125k 500\nL352@P4: 1M 40\nL353@P4: USB (low -speed, 1.1) 1.5M 3 cable specs 2 bus specs\nL354@P4: USB(full -speed, 1.1) 1.5/12M\nL355@P4: Hi-Spe ed USB (2.0) 480M\nL356@P4: IEEE-1394 100 to 400M+ 72 16 hops, 4.5M each 63 6-bit address\nL357@P4: CAN diff erential\nL358@P4: 25\nL359@P4: propagation delays\nL360@P4: 5 cables linking 6 nodes\nL361@P4: (5m cable node to node) 127 bus and hub specs\nL362@P4: 100\nL363@P4: load resistance and\nL364@P4: transceiver current\nL365@P4: drive\nL366@P4: Slide 10\nL367@P4: In Slide 10 we look at three important characteristics:\nL368@P4: • Speed, or data rate\nL369@P4: • Number of devices allowed to be connected (to\nL370@P4: share the bus wires)\nL371@P4: • Total length of the wiring\nL372@P4: Numbers are supposed to be realistic estimates but are\nL373@P4: based on meeting bus specifications. But rules are made\nL374@P4: to be broken! When buffered, I2\nL375@P4: C can be limited by\nL376@P4: wiring propagation delays but it is still possible to run\nL377@P4: much longer distances by using slower clock rates and\nL378@P4: maybe also compromising the bus rise and fall-time\nL379@P4: specifications on the buffered bus because it is not\nL380@P4: bound to conform to I2C specifications.\nL381@P4: The figure in Slide 10 limiting I2\nL382@P4: C range by\nL383@P4: propagation delays is conservative and allows for\nL384@P4: published response delays in chips like older E2\nL385@P4: memories. Measured chip responses are typically <\nL386@P4: 700 ns and that allows for long cable delays and/or\nL387@P4-5: 5 AN10216-01 I2\nL388@P5: C Manual\nL389@P5: operation well above 100 kHz with the P82B96. The\nL390@P5: theoretical round-trip delay on 100 m of cable is only\nL391@P5: approx 1 µs and the maximum allowed delay, assuming\nL392@P5: zero delays in ICs, is about 3 µs at 100 kHz. The\nL393@P5: figures for CAN are not quite as conservative; they are\nL394@P5: the ‘often quoted values’. The round trip delay in 10\nL395@P5: km cable is about 0.1 ms while 5 kbps implies 0.2 ms\nL396@P5: nominal bit time, and a need to sample during the\nL397@P5: second half of the bit time. That is under the user’s\nL398@P5: control, but needs attention.\nL399@P5: USB 2 and IEEE-1394 are still ‘emerging standards’.\nL400@P5: Figures quoted may not be practical; they are just based\nL401@P5: on the specification restrictions.\nL402@P5: UART Overview\nL403@P5: DesignCon 2003 TecForum I\nL404@P5: 2C Bus Overview 11\nL405@P5: What is UART?\nL406@P5: (Universal Asynchronous Receiver Transmitter)\nL407@P5: • Communication standard implemented in the 60’s.\nL408@P5: • Simple, universal, well understood and well supported.\nL409@P5: • Slow speed communication standard: up to 1 Mbits/s\nL410@P5: • Asynchronous means that the data clock is not included in\nL411@P5: the data: Sender and Receiver must agree on timing\nL412@P5: parameters in advance.\nL413@P5: • “Start” and “Stop” bits indicates the data to be sent\nL414@P5: • Parity information can also be sent\nL415@P5: Start bit\nL416@P5: Parity Information\nL417@P5: 8 Bit Data\nL418@P5: 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7\nL419@P5: Stop bit\nL420@P5: Slide 11\nL421@P5: UARTs (Universal Asynchronous Receiver\nL422@P5: Transmitter) are serial chips on your PC motherboard\nL423@P5: (or on an internal modem card). The UART function\nL424@P5: may also be done on a chip that does other things as\nL425@P5: well. On older computers like many 486's, the chips\nL426@P5: were on the disk IO controller card. Still older\nL427@P5: computers have dedicated serial boards.\nL428@P5: The UARTs purpose is to convert bytes from the PC's\nL429@P5: parallel bus to a serial bit-stream. The cable going out\nL430@P5: of the serial port is serial and has only one wire for each\nL431@P5: direction of flow. The serial port sends out a stream of\nL432@P5: bits, one bit at a time. Conversely, the bit stream that\nL433@P5: enters the serial port via the external cable is converted\nL434@P5: to parallel bytes that the computer can understand.\nL435@P5: UARTs deal with data in byte-sized pieces, which is\nL436@P5: conveniently also the size of ASCII characters.\nL437@P5: Say you have a terminal hooked up to your PC. When\nL438@P5: you type a character, the terminal gives that character to\nL439@P5: its transmitter (also a UART). The transmitter sends\nL440@P5: that byte out onto the serial line, one bit at a time, at a\nL441@P5: specific rate. On the PC end, the receiving UART takes\nL442@P5: all the bits and rebuilds the (parallel) byte and puts it in\nL443@P5: a buffer.\nL444@P5: Along with converting between serial and parallel, the\nL445@P5: UART does some other things as a byproduct (side\nL446@P5: effect) of its primary task. The voltage used to represent\nL447@P5: bits is also converted (changed). Extra bits (called start\nL448@P5: and stop bits) are added to each byte before it is\nL449@P5: transmitted. Also, while the flow rate (in bytes/s) on the\nL450@P5: parallel bus speed inside the computer is very high, the\nL451@P5: flow rate out the UART on the serial port side of it is\nL452@P5: much lower. The UART has a fixed set of rates\nL453@P5: (speeds) that it can use at its serial port interface.\nL454@P5: DesignCon 2003 TecForum I\nL455@P5: 2C Bus Overview 12\nL456@P5: UART - Applications\nL457@P5: Client\nL458@P5: Processor\nL459@P5: Client\nL460@P5: Processor\nL461@P5: Datacom\nL462@P5: controller\nL463@P5: Datacom\nL464@P5: controller\nL465@P5: t\nL466@P5: r\nL467@P5: x\nL468@P5: t\nL469@P5: r\nL470@P5: x\nL471@P5: Server\nL472@P5: Processor\nL473@P5: Server\nL474@P5: Processor Digital Parallel\nL475@P5: Interface\nL476@P5: t\nL477@P5: r\nL478@P5: x\nL479@P5: t\nL480@P5: r\nL481@P5: x\nL482@P5: Datacom\nL483@P5: controller\nL484@P5: Datacom\nL485@P5: controller\nL486@P5: LAN application\nL487@P5: al Interf\nL488@P5: Cash\nL489@P5: register\nL490@P5: Micro\nL491@P5: contr.\nL492@P5: Micro\nL493@P5: contr.\nL494@P5: DUART\nL495@P5: SC28L92\nL496@P5: DUART\nL497@P5: SC28L92\nL498@P5: Memory Memory\nL499@P5: Address\nL500@P5: Data\nL501@P5: Display\nL502@P5: Bar code\nL503@P5: reader\nL504@P5: UART\nL505@P5: Printer\nL506@P5: Interface to\nL507@P5: Server\nL508@P5: Appliance Terminals\nL509@P5: • Entertainment\nL510@P5: • Home Security\nL511@P5: • Robotics\nL512@P5: • Automotive\nL513@P5: • Cellular\nL514@P5: • Medical\nL515@P5: MoModdeemm\nL516@P5: t\nL517@P5: r\nL518@P5: x\nL519@P5: t\nL520@P5: r\nL521@P5: x\nL522@P5: Analog or Digital MoMode demm\nL523@P5: t\nL524@P5: r\nL525@P5: x\nL526@P5: Public / Private\nL527@P5: Telephone / Internet\nL528@P5: Network\nL529@P5: WAN application\nL530@P5: Seri ace\nL531@P5: Serial Interface\nL532@P5: Slide 12\nL533@P5: SPI Overview\nL534@P5: DesignCon 2003 TecForum I\nL535@P5: 2C Bus Overview 13\nL536@P5: What is SPI?\nL537@P5: • Serial Peripheral Interface (SPI) is a 4-wire full-duplex\nL538@P5: synchronous serial data link:\nL539@P5: – SCLK: Serial Clock\nL540@P5: – MOSI: Master Out Slave In - Data from Master to Slave\nL541@P5: – MISO: Master In Slave Out - Data from Slave to Master\nL542@P5: – SS: Slave Select\nL543@P5: • Originally developed by Motorola\nL544@P5: • Used for connecting peripherals to each other and to\nL545@P5: microprocessors\nL546@P5: • Shift register that serially transmits data to other SPI devices\nL547@P5: • Actually a “3 + n” wire interface with n = number of devices\nL548@P5: • Only one master active at a time\nL549@P5: • Various Speed transfers (function of the system clock)\nL550@P5: Slide 13\nL551@P5: The Serial Peripheral Interface (SPI) circuit is a\nL552@P5: synchronous serial data link that is standard across\nL553@P5: many Motorola microprocessors and other peripheral\nL554@P5: chips. It provides support for a high bandwidth (1 mega\nL555@P5: baud) network connection amongst CPUs and other\nL556@P5: devices supporting the SPI.\nL557@P5-6: 6 AN10216-01 I2\nL558@P6: C Manual\nL559@P6: DesignCon 2003 TecForum I\nL560@P6: 2C Bus Overview 14\nL561@P6: SCLK\nL562@P6: MOSI\nL563@P6: MISO\nL564@P6: SS\nL565@P6: SLAVE 1\nL566@P6: 